#THE TASK
#When texting or tweeting, it’s not uncommon to shorten words to save time or space, as by omitting vowels, much like Twitter was originally called twttr
#In a file called twttr.py, implement a program that prompts the user for a str of text and then outputs that same text but with all vowels (A, E, I, O, and U) omitted, whether inputted in uppercase or lowercase

#THOUGHTS...
#We can examine a string one character at a time and build a new one to print
#We will know the string length so we can use a for loop (Python will have a collection of characters to check)
#We must implement a check/condition to either keep or remove certain characters (remove vowels)
#We can create a variable containing a list of safe_characters or to_be_removed_characters
#We transfer the safe vowels into a new string to print

#The solution architecture is something like:
#Ask the user for text
#Create an empty results string
#For each character in the string check aeiou and add remainder to new string 

#SOLUTION

#Ask the user for input and store as variable
original = input("What would you like to post?")

#Create new string to be updated 
new = "" 

#Create variable of list of to be removed characters
to_be_removed = ["a","e","i","o","u"] #Individual string values need "" - otherwise python assumes they are variable names

#Create the main logic in a for loop 
for character in original: #Characters is our placeholder variable while the loop iterrates over each letter stored in the string variable original 
    if character.lower()in to_be_removed: #Implementing our conditional with the list of vowels stored as variable earlier - we add .lower() to match the character to the lowercase values in the list we created 
    #If we used if character == ... we would check if one character equals the entire list 
        pass #If the character is in the list we do nothing -> we don't put it in the new/to be printed string 
    else:
        new = new + character 

#Print the final/new version of the post

print(f"New Tweet: {new}")


